-- Q1: medium / L3 — Market-based sourcing vs cost-of-performance for services
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Ridgemont Consulting LLC, headquartered in State A, performs data analytics services for a client located in State B. All work is performed by employees in State A, and the service benefit is received entirely in State B. State B uses market-based sourcing for its sales factor. How should Ridgemont source this service revenue for State B apportionment purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The revenue is sourced to State B because the customer received the benefit there", "The revenue is sourced to State A because all work was performed there", "The revenue is split equally between State A and State B", "The revenue is excluded from the sales factor because it is service income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under market-based sourcing rules adopted by a growing majority of states, service revenue is sourced to the state where the customer receives the benefit of the service, regardless of where the service provider performs the work. State B sources this revenue to itself because the benefit is delivered there. Wrong (B): This describes cost-of-performance sourcing, which is the older UDITPA approach. State B has adopted market-based sourcing, so the location of performance is irrelevant. Wrong (C): No apportionment rule splits individual service receipts equally between states; revenue is sourced based on either market or cost-of-performance. Wrong (D): Service income is included in the sales factor under both market-based and cost-of-performance sourcing methods.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — State addback statutes for related-party intangible expenses
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Waverly Corp pays $4 million in royalties to its wholly owned subsidiary in a state with no corporate income tax for use of a trademark. Waverly files returns in State G, which has an intangible expense addback statute. How does State G treat the royalty deduction on Waverly's state return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["State G allows the full deduction because the royalty is an ordinary business expense", "State G requires Waverly to add back the royalty payment to its taxable income unless an exception applies", "State G disallows only the portion exceeding an arm's length amount", "State G requires the subsidiary to file a combined return instead of applying an addback"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): States with intangible expense addback statutes require taxpayers to add back deductions for royalties, management fees, and similar payments made to related parties in low-tax or no-tax jurisdictions. Limited exceptions may apply if the taxpayer can demonstrate the transaction has economic substance or a valid business purpose. Wrong (A): Addback statutes specifically override the ordinary deduction for related-party intangible payments to prevent income shifting. Wrong (C): Addback statutes disallow the entire deduction by default, not just the amount exceeding arm's length; the arm's length standard is a transfer pricing concept under IRC Section 482. Wrong (D): Addback statutes and combined reporting are separate anti-avoidance mechanisms; a state may use one or both.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — UDITPA model act purpose
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$What is the primary purpose of the Uniform Division of Income for Tax Purposes Act (UDITPA)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To establish federal nexus standards for state taxation", "To require all states to adopt combined reporting", "To provide a uniform framework for dividing a multistate business's income among states", "To set maximum state corporate income tax rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): UDITPA is a model act adopted in whole or in part by most states. It provides a uniform framework for classifying income as business or nonbusiness and for apportioning business income among states using a formulary method based on property, payroll, and sales factors. Wrong (A): UDITPA addresses division of income, not nexus standards. Nexus is governed by due process and commerce clause analysis. Wrong (B): UDITPA does not mandate combined reporting; combined reporting is a separate state-level policy choice. Wrong (D): UDITPA does not set tax rates. Each state independently determines its own corporate income tax rate.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Allocation vs apportionment distinction
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$In multistate taxation, what distinguishes allocation from apportionment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Allocation uses a formula while apportionment assigns income to a single state", "Allocation applies only to foreign-source income while apportionment applies to domestic income", "Allocation assigns nonbusiness income to a specific state while apportionment divides business income among states by formula", "Allocation and apportionment are interchangeable terms for dividing income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under UDITPA and most state tax systems, allocation assigns specific items of nonbusiness income (such as rents from real property or gains from the sale of a fixed asset) directly to a particular state, typically the commercial domicile or situs state. Apportionment divides business income among all states where the taxpayer has nexus using a formulary method. Wrong (A): This reverses the definitions. Apportionment uses a formula; allocation assigns to a specific state. Wrong (B): Both allocation and apportionment apply to domestic multistate income, not foreign-source income. Wrong (D): Allocation and apportionment are distinct concepts with different rules and applications.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — State bonus depreciation decoupling
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Brennan Manufacturing places $2 million of qualifying equipment in service during 2026 and claims 60% federal bonus depreciation under IRC Section 168(k). Brennan files in State L, which has decoupled from bonus depreciation and requires use of the pre-bonus MACRS schedule. What adjustment must Brennan make on its State L return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Add back the bonus depreciation amount and substitute the regular MACRS deduction for the year", "Claim the full federal bonus depreciation with no adjustment", "Deduct 50% of the bonus depreciation as a compromise", "Carry the bonus depreciation forward over five years on the state return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): When a state decouples from IRC Section 168(k) bonus depreciation, the taxpayer must add back the excess depreciation claimed federally and instead deduct only the regular MACRS depreciation allowable for that year. This creates a timing difference as the deduction is spread over the asset's recovery period for state purposes. Wrong (B): States that decouple do not allow the federal bonus amount; an adjustment is required on the state return. Wrong (C): Decoupled states do not allow a partial bonus deduction. The entire bonus amount is disallowed and replaced with standard MACRS. Wrong (D): There is no five-year carryforward mechanism. The taxpayer simply claims regular MACRS depreciation each year over the asset's recovery period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Joyce rule vs Finnigan rule in combined returns
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Crossfield Group files a combined return in State M, which follows the Finnigan rule. Member A has nexus in State M and $3 million in State M sales. Member B has no nexus in State M but has $5 million in sales to State M customers. The combined group's total everywhere sales are $40 million. The tax advisor is evaluating whether the Finnigan rule or a Joyce-rule state would produce a lower apportionment percentage. What is the combined group's State M sales factor under the Finnigan rule, and how would a Joyce-rule state differ?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["20.0% under Finnigan; a Joyce-rule state would include only Member A's $3 million for a 7.5% factor", "12.5% under Finnigan; a Joyce-rule state would exclude all sales because nexus is not unanimous", "7.5% under Finnigan; a Joyce-rule state would include both members' sales for a 20.0% factor", "20.0% under Finnigan; a Joyce-rule state would also be 20.0% because combined filing eliminates the distinction"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Finnigan rule, all sales destined for the filing state are included in the sales factor numerator regardless of whether the selling member individually has nexus there. Thus ($3M + $5M) / $40M = 20.0%. Under the Joyce rule, only sales by members that individually have nexus in the state are included in the numerator, so $3M / $40M = 7.5%. This distinction can significantly affect the combined group's state tax liability. Wrong (B): The 12.5% figure has no computational basis, and the Joyce rule does not exclude all sales — it includes sales by members with nexus. Wrong (C): This reverses the two rules. Finnigan includes all members' sales; Joyce restricts to nexus members only. Wrong (D): The Finnigan and Joyce rules produce different results precisely because they treat non-nexus member sales differently within combined returns.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Water's-edge vs worldwide combined reporting election
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Orion Holdings, a U.S. parent with domestic subsidiaries and a highly profitable foreign subsidiary in a low-tax jurisdiction, files combined returns in State N. State N requires worldwide combined reporting by default but allows a water's-edge election. The foreign subsidiary has substantial sales but no U.S. nexus. Orion's tax director must decide whether to elect water's-edge treatment. Which analysis best evaluates the election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Water's-edge always reduces state tax because it excludes foreign income from the combined group", "Worldwide reporting is always preferable because it increases the denominator of the apportionment formula", "Water's-edge excludes the foreign subsidiary's income and factors, which may increase or decrease Orion's State N tax depending on the subsidiary's relative factor proportions", "The election is irrelevant because State N cannot constitutionally tax foreign subsidiary income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A water's-edge election excludes foreign entities from the combined group, removing both their income and their apportionment factors. If the foreign subsidiary's factors (especially sales) would dilute the State N apportionment percentage, excluding it increases the percentage applied to a smaller base. The net effect depends on the relative magnitudes of excluded income versus excluded factors. Wrong (A): Water's-edge does not always reduce tax. If the foreign subsidiary's large sales denominator was diluting State N's factor, excluding it can increase the apportionment percentage enough to raise total state tax. Wrong (B): A larger denominator reduces the apportionment percentage but applies to a larger combined income base; the interaction is not always favorable. Wrong (D): States with worldwide combined reporting have been upheld constitutionally. The U.S. Supreme Court in Container Corp. v. Franchise Tax Board (1983) and Barclays Bank v. Franchise Tax Board (1994) affirmed this authority.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Voluntary disclosure agreements for historical nexus exposure
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Keystone Digital LLC has been selling software to customers in State P for four years without filing returns there. After the Wayfair decision, the company's tax advisor determines that Keystone exceeded State P's economic nexus thresholds in each of those years. The advisor recommends entering a voluntary disclosure agreement (VDA) with State P. What is the primary benefit of a VDA compared to waiting for State P to initiate an assessment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A VDA eliminates all past tax liability and starts the filing obligation prospectively", "A VDA guarantees a refund of any sales tax collected from customers during the exposure period", "A VDA allows the taxpayer to register and resolve past exposure before discovery, typically with reduced or waived penalties and a limited lookback period", "A VDA permanently exempts the taxpayer from audit by the participating state"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Voluntary disclosure agreements, often facilitated through the Multistate Tax Commission, allow taxpayers to come forward before a state initiates contact. In exchange for voluntary compliance, the state typically waives penalties and limits the lookback period (often to three or four years), reducing the total exposure compared to a state-initiated audit. Wrong (A): A VDA does not eliminate past tax liability. The taxpayer must still pay back taxes and interest for the agreed lookback period. Wrong (B): VDAs address the taxpayer's filing and payment obligations, not refunds. Any overcollected tax would be a separate matter. Wrong (D): A VDA does not grant audit immunity. The state retains the right to audit the taxpayer for periods and issues outside the VDA scope.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — Nonbusiness income allocation to commercial domicile
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Whitfield Corp, domiciled in State Q, earns $200,000 in interest from a passive investment unrelated to its regular business operations. Whitfield has nexus in three states. Under UDITPA principles, to which state is this nonbusiness interest income allocated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["State Q, the taxpayer's commercial domicile", "Equally among all three states where Whitfield has nexus", "The state where the investment issuer is located", "It is apportioned using the standard three-factor formula"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UDITPA Section 7, interest income classified as nonbusiness income is allocated to the taxpayer's commercial domicile. Nonbusiness income is not apportioned by formula but instead assigned directly to a specific state based on the nature of the income and the taxpayer's domicile. Wrong (B): Nonbusiness income is not divided among nexus states. It is allocated entirely to one state based on defined rules. Wrong (C): The situs of the issuer is not the allocation factor for interest income. UDITPA allocates nonbusiness interest to the taxpayer's commercial domicile. Wrong (D): Only business income is apportioned by formula. Nonbusiness income is allocated, not apportioned.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — State NOL carryforward limitation differences
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Fielding Corp has a $500,000 federal net operating loss (NOL) carryforward. The company files in State R, which limits NOL deductions to 50% of taxable income per year and does not allow carryback. For federal purposes, the NOL deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income. Fielding has $600,000 of state taxable income before the NOL deduction. What is the maximum NOL deduction Fielding can claim on its State R return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$480,000, applying the federal 80% limitation", "$300,000, applying State R's 50% limitation to taxable income", "$500,000, because the full carryforward is available", "$250,000, applying the 50% limitation to the NOL rather than taxable income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): State R limits the NOL deduction to 50% of the taxpayer's state taxable income. Applying this to $600,000 yields a maximum deduction of $300,000. The remaining $200,000 of the NOL carryforward continues to future years. States commonly impose their own NOL limitations independent of the federal 80% cap under IRC Section 172. Wrong (A): The federal 80% limitation applies for federal purposes. State R has its own 50% cap that overrides the federal rule on the state return. Wrong (C): Although $500,000 is available, State R's percentage limitation restricts the amount deductible in any single year. Wrong (D): The 50% limitation applies to taxable income, not to the NOL balance itself. 50% of $600,000 taxable income is $300,000.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Static conformity date and federal tax law changes
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Pendleton Corp operates in State S, which uses static conformity and conforms to the Internal Revenue Code as of January 1, 2024. Congress enacted a new above-the-line deduction effective for tax year 2025. Pendleton claims this deduction on its 2025 federal return. When preparing its State S return for 2025, how should Pendleton treat this deduction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Claim the deduction because it is allowed on the federal return", "Claim the deduction only if State S has issued specific guidance adopting it", "Add back the deduction because State S's static conformity date predates the enactment, so the deduction does not exist for state purposes", "Defer the deduction until State S updates its conformity date, then file an amended return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under static conformity, a state adopts the IRC as of a fixed date and does not automatically incorporate subsequent federal changes. Because State S conforms to the IRC as of January 1, 2024, a deduction enacted in 2025 does not exist for state tax purposes until the legislature updates the conformity date. Pendleton must add back the deduction on its State S return. Wrong (A): Static conformity means federal law changes after the conformity date are not automatically recognized. The federal allowance does not control state treatment. Wrong (B): Under static conformity, specific guidance is not needed — the deduction is simply not part of the adopted code. The state would need to update its conformity date legislatively. Wrong (D): There is no deferral mechanism. The taxpayer must file under the law as it stands; if conformity is later updated, an amended return may be optional but is not required or automatic.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

